1 OCCU stone mason
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} According to the June 14, 1917 Kanabec County Times, front page.  George Amundson obituary.  
2 CONT
2 CONT The times is this week called upon to chronicle the death of one of the prominent citizens of Knife lake township, George Amundson, who died on Sunday June 10th, at 11:10 in the forenoon at a hopsital in Minneapolis, folliwing an 
2 CONC operation for gall stones and cancer.  The remains were brot to Mora Monday evening.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Deceased was born December 27th, 1859 at Christainsen, Norway, where he resided during his boyhood days.  In 1881 he immigrated to America, coming to Kanabec County and settling in the township of Knife Lake twenty years ago last 
2 CONC May.  Mr. Amundson had been ailing for the past ten years, but was able to follow his trade, that of a stone mason.   
2 CONT
2 CONT On February 15th, 1883, he was married to Miss Tillie Abrahamson in LaSalle county, Illinois, and from this union twelve children were born, three of whom are dead.  He leaves to survive him, a widow and nine children, namely; Ben
2 CONC nie, Mollie, Mandy, Albert, Olga, Carl, Oscar, Julia and Alice, also two brothers, Ben, who resides at Knife lake, and Sam who resides in Canada.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Funeral services were held yesterday afternoon from the home and at the Knife Lake Swedish Baptist church Re. Jensen of the Norwegian Lutheran church officiating.  Interment was made in the Knife Lake cemetery.  
